## Verify Configuration Before Boot

Before booting compute nodes, verify that boot-service and metadata-service are
configured correctly. These checks catch the most common failures before a
wasted boot cycle.

First, confirm that boot-service has the compute boot configuration, with the
`ds=nocloud-net` kernel parameter that points the nodes at cloud-init:

```bash
ochami -k boot config list -F yaml
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify boot-service boot config is uploaded -->
<!-- ohpc_command ochami -k boot config list -F yaml | grep -q "ds=nocloud-net" -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the cloud-init defaults were uploaded; the output includes
`base_url`:

```bash
ochami -k metadata defaults list -F yaml
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init defaults are uploaded -->
<!-- ohpc_command ochami -k metadata defaults list -F yaml | grep -q "base_url" -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the `compute` group is present. This is what delivers the SSH
keys and munge key to the node at boot:

```bash
ochami -k metadata group list -F yaml
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init compute group is uploaded -->
<!-- ohpc_command ochami -k metadata group list -F yaml | grep -qE "^ +name: compute$" -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Finally, confirm that the boot-service and the metadata-service know each
compute node. These are the lookups the node itself performs at boot, and the
only checks here that have to run after node discovery.

Both services answer from their own copy of SMD's nodes, which they refresh on a
timer, so a node registered a moment ago is not known to them yet. A node that
boots into that window gets no boot script, or no cloud-init data, which means
no root SSH key and no munge key, and it never becomes reachable. Continue
after both services know every node.

The meta-data check asks the metadata-service the question the node will ask,
with `X-Forwarded-For` carrying the node's address so that the server resolves
the same entry. It asks the server's container directly rather than through
haproxy, because haproxy replaces `X-Forwarded-For` with the address the
request came from, so through it the server would look up the head node
instead. The node's own request through haproxy is tested when it boots. The
container's address exists only on the head node, so the checks tell curl to
ignore an `http_proxy` set in the environment; a proxy cannot reach the podman
network and the request would time out there.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Find the metadata-service's container address -->
```bash
metadata_ip=$(podman inspect --format \
	'{%raw%}{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.IPAddress}}{{end}}{%endraw%}' \
	metadata-service)
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

To check one node, the boot script should have a `kernel` line, and the
meta-data the node's own `instance-id`:

```bash
curl -fsS --noproxy '*' "http://${sms_ip}:8081/boot-service/bootscript?mac=${c_mac[0]}"
curl -fsS --noproxy '*' -H "X-Forwarded-For: ${c_ip[0]}" \
	"http://${metadata_ip}:8080/meta-data"
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Wait for the boot-service and metadata-service to resolve each compute node -->
<!-- ohpc_command for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)); do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     for ((t=0; ; t++)); do -->
<!-- ohpc_command         boot=0; meta=0 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         curl -fsS -m 5 --noproxy '*' "http://${sms_ip}:8081/boot-service/bootscript?mac=${c_mac[$i]}" \ -->
<!-- ohpc_command             | grep -q "^kernel " && boot=1 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         curl -fsS -m 5 --noproxy '*' -H "X-Forwarded-For: ${c_ip[$i]}" "http://${metadata_ip}:8080/meta-data" \ -->
<!-- ohpc_command             | grep -qx "instance-id: x1000c0s0b$((i+1))n0" && meta=1 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         (( boot && meta )) && break -->
<!-- ohpc_command         (( t < 132 )) || { echo "pre-boot node: FAILED for ${c_name[$i]} (boot-service=${boot} metadata-service=${meta})"; exit 1; } -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "Waiting for ${c_name[$i]}: boot-service=${boot} metadata-service=${meta}" -->
<!-- ohpc_command         sleep 5 -->
<!-- ohpc_command     done -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_command echo "pre-boot node: OK" -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->
